<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    bg_attach_option — circular option-tile background
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - bottom_sheet_attach.xml (5 × FrameLayout circles,
        one per media type)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. THEME ATTRIBUTE FILL.
       The <solid> uses "?attr/colorSurfaceVariant", which
       resolves through the active theme:
         - light theme → @color/md_surface_variant_light
                         (#EFECF4, a very light lavender-gray)
         - dark theme  → @color/md_surface_variant_dark
                         (#2A2831, a deep desaturated purple)

       Both values are the "surface variant" token from our
       color palette — the standard Material 3 role for
       "container backgrounds that hold grouped content."
       A single drawable file serves both themes with zero
       drawable-night/ duplication.

       Do NOT change to a fixed @color/... reference. The
       surface-variant color is designed to sit one elevation
       step above the surface (which is the sheet's own
       background) — using a fixed color would break that
       relationship in one of the two themes.

    2. OVAL SHAPE.
       android:shape="oval" — the consumer FrameLayout declares
       56dp × 56dp, so the oval renders as a perfect circle
       (an oval in a square bounds is a circle). This is the
       same construction as bg_avatar_initial and
       bg_brand_dot — three files, same shape, different
       purposes. Do NOT try to consolidate them into a single
       "bg_circle.xml" with a theme attribute fill — the three
       need different fills, and a single shape with a fixed
       fill would need to be tinted at each consumer, which
       would add a runtime tint call for a static property.

    3. NO STROKE, NO GRADIENT, NO PADDING.
       The circle is a flat-filled shape. Adding a stroke
       would make it look like a radio button or a selected
       state. Adding a gradient at 56dp is invisible on most
       screens. Adding <padding> would shrink the circle
       within its bounds, producing a smaller-diameter
       circle than the icon inside it — which would look
       wrong.

    4. NO <size>.
       Shape drawables are sized by their consumer View's
       bounds. Adding <size android:width="56dp"
       android:height="56dp" /> would set an intrinsic size
       that the FrameLayout would then have to override,
       producing subtle inconsistencies on some OEMs.
       The FrameLayout's own 56dp × 56dp declaration is
       the single source of truth for the circle's size.

    5. NO android:tint ON THE DRAWABLE.
       Same discipline as every shape drawable in the
       project: the fill is set via ?attr/colorSurfaceVariant
       (resolved through the theme), NOT via a runtime
       setBackgroundTintList. A tint would layer on top of
       the themed color, producing a slightly different hue
       than the theme intends.

    Rendering result at 56dp on a surface background:
      A subtle, low-contrast circular tile that holds the
      icon, visually grouping the icon into a tappable
      affordance without competing with the icon's own
      color.
    ============================================================
-->
<shape
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="oval">

    <solid android:color="?attr/colorSurfaceVariant" />

</shape>
